@startuml
title ECS Engine Prototype - ECS Architecture

left to right direction

skinparam componentStyle rectangle
skinparam shadowing false
skinparam packageStyle rectangle
skinparam linetype ortho

package "ECS" {
  [Registry] as Registry
  [Entity Manager] as EntityManager
  [View / Query API\nview<T...>().each(...)] as ViewApi
  [Component Storage Set] as StorageSet
  [System Manager] as SystemManager
}

package "Component Types" {
  [Transform] as Transform
  [Velocity] as Velocity
  [Boid] as Boid
  [Renderable] as Renderable
}

package "Systems" {
  [Boid System] as BoidSystem
  [Movement System] as MovementSystem
  [Render System] as RenderSystem
}

Registry --> EntityManager : creates / destroys entities
Registry --> ViewApi : creates typed views
Registry --> StorageSet : owns typed storages
Registry --> SystemManager : registers systems

ViewApi --> StorageSet : iterates matching components

StorageSet --> Transform
StorageSet --> Velocity
StorageSet --> Boid
StorageSet --> Renderable

SystemManager --> BoidSystem
SystemManager --> MovementSystem
SystemManager --> RenderSystem

BoidSystem --> Registry : requests view
MovementSystem --> Registry : requests view
RenderSystem --> Registry : requests view

BoidSystem --> ViewApi : View<Transform, Velocity, Boid>
MovementSystem --> ViewApi : View<Transform, Velocity>
RenderSystem --> ViewApi : View<Transform, Renderable>

note bottom of Registry
  Registry is the public ECS access point.
  Systems use typed views instead of accessing
  sparse-set storage internals directly.
end note

note bottom of ViewApi
  Initial implementation can use sparse-set storage.
  The public system API can remain stable if storage
  later changes to an archetype or chunk layout.
end note

@enduml
